Hi Avrian,

 

Please find attached the calculations for the Yupi IPO along with the IPO
prospectus. Couldn't find any coverage on this so just relying on the
prospectus + what I found. Book building from Mar 17 - 19 (CIMB Niaga
Sekuritas and Mandiri Sekuritas underwriting).

 

IPO low / high end price of 2,100 / 2,500 IDR implying mkt cap USD of 1.1bn
/ 1.31bn. Sales in FY24e could come in at US$183mn with PATMI of US$37.7mn
(FY24e / 25e P/E of 34.7x / 32.35x at the high end). Slight net cash of 2-3%
market cap post IPO. 

 

Use of proceeds:

New factory in East Java with a total estimated cost of RP437.5bn
(US$26.8mn) which will come into operation earliest in 2026. The rest will
be for working capital. Current net PPE on balance sheet is at 1.3trillion
rupiah so the new factory implies a 33.7% expansion in capacity (assuming
linearity). 

 

Market share:

Indonesia soft candy market is concentrated, Yupi commands 66.5% in 2024
with the 2nd - 5th player having 9.2%, 5.3%, 3.8% and 3.1% of the market.
Top 5 makes up 87.9% of the market of which 3 are local Indonesian brands
while other 2 are from Europe. 

 

Yupi Market shares for other markets:

Thailand: No. 1 at 23.4%

Malaysia: No. 2 at 21.1% (Leader at 21.8%)

Singapore: No. 1 at 17.2% (2nd player at 14.8%)

 

Indonesia distribution:

Distribution channel similar to Delfi, dominated by traditional trade (65.3%
of Yupi sales) over 3.9mn small local grocery stores spread across
neighborhoods, near schools, parks etc. The Yupi brand is available in 27.5%
of traditional market outlets (they are in 1 in 4 small grocery stores) and
they think they can expand their presence by targeting the other small
stores. The rest of revenue comes from modern markets (33.9% of sales)
largely minimarts. Supermarkets and hypermarkets are positioned for larger
less frequent family shopping trips and hence offer larger pack sizes (to
provide greater value). E-commerce contributed only 0.8% of total sales
value as dominance of impulse buying for their products limits the appeal of
online channels.

 

Dividend:

Post-IPO, mgmt. is committed to distributing dividends of up to 80% of net
profit. Don't think they will go to that level but at 80% payout, div yield
at the high end of IPO range is around 2.3%. 

 

Summary:

IDX Composite down 5.95% YTD and Mayora Indah down 18+% YTD. Mayora is now
trading at 14+x FY25e P/E. There is probably more info available on growth
plans during the roadshow but just based on the prospectus, Yupi doesn't
appear to be attractive based on numbers at IPO price (25e 32.35x P/E). Even
with an assumed 30% capacity expansion leading to a 30% cliff up in PATMI
post 2026, its still around 26x earnings multiple at its high-end price. 

 

Shares could still pop given tight supply - total IPO 10% of outstanding
shares of which 7% vendor shares (major shareholder, Sweets Indonesia) and
3% new shares.

 

 


